Here is the complete step-by-step solution:


To find the base 7 representation of $629_{10}$, we will repeatedly divide $629_{10}$ by 7 and keep track of the remainders.


Step 1: Divide $629_{10}$ by 7


\begin{align*}
\frac{629}{7} &= 89 \text{ remainder }6 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 629 & = 7(89)+6
\end{align*}

Step 2: Repeatedly divide 89 by 7


\begin{align*}
\frac{89}{7} &= 12 \text{ remainder }5 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 89 & = 7(12)+5
\end{align*}

Step 3: Repeatedly divide 12 by 7


\begin{align*}
\frac{12}{7} &= 1 \text{ remainder }5 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 12 & = 7(1)+5
\end{align*}

Step 4: Repeatedly divide 1 by 7


\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{7} &= 0 \text{ remainder }1 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 1 & = 7(0)+1
\end{align*}

Now that we have the complete division, we can write the base 7 representation of $629_{10}$ by writing the remainders from bottom to top:


$$\underline{1\;5\;5\;6}$$


Now that we have the base 7 representation, we need to convert $629_{10}$ to base 8. To do this, we will repeatedly divide $629_{10}$ by 8 and keep track of the remainders.


Step 5: Divide $629_{10}$ by 8


\begin{align*}
\frac{629}{8} &= 78 \text{ remainder } 9 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 629 & = 8(78)+9
\end{align*}

Step 6: Repeatedly divide 78 by 8


\begin{align*}
\frac{78}{8} &= 9 \text{ remainder }6 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 78 & = 8(9)+6
\end{align*}

Step 7: Repeatedly divide 9 by 8


\begin{align*}
\frac{9}{8} &= 1 \text{ remainder }1 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 9 & = 8(1)+1
\end{align*}

Step 8: Repeatedly divide 1 by 8


\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{8} &= 0 \text{ remainder }1 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 1 & = 8(0)+1
\end{align*}

Now that we have the complete division, we can write the base 8 representation of $629_{10}$ by writing the remainders from bottom to top:


$$\underline{1\;6\;9}$$


Comparing the base 7 and base 8 representations, we see that they share the digits 1 and 5.


The answer is \boxed{2}.

