**Problem Statement**

Given the expression \[2\sqrt{2} + 6\sqrt{3} + 3\sqrt{6},\] express it in the form $a\sqrt{2} + b\sqrt{3} + c\sqrt{6}$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are integers. Find the value of $a + b + c$.

**Step 1:** Expand the given expression using the distributive property.

\begin{align*}
\left(2\sqrt{2} + 6\sqrt{3} + 3\sqrt{6}\right)&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+6\sqrt{3}+3\sqrt{2\cdot3}\\
&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+18\sqrt{3}+3\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

**Step 2:** Simplify the $\sqrt{6}$ term.

\begin{align*}
\sqrt{6}&amp;=\sqrt{2\cdot3}\\
&amp;=\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

**Step 3:** Substitute the simplified $\sqrt{6}$ term into the expanded expression and combine like terms.

\begin{align*}
2\sqrt{2}+18\sqrt{3}+3\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{3}&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+18\sqrt{3}+3\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{3}\\
&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+18\sqrt{3}+3\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{3}\\
&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+(18+3)\sqrt{3}\\
&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+21\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

**Step 4:** Express the simplified expression in the desired form.

\begin{align*}
2\sqrt{2}+21\sqrt{3}&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+21\sqrt{3}\\
&amp;=2\sqrt{2}+21\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

**Step 5:** Evaluate the sum $a + b + c$.

\begin{align*}
a+b+c &amp;=2+21\\
&amp;=\boxed{23}
\end{align*}

The answer is \boxed{11}.
